oints that actually got  labeled so so now let's look at you know  what we got to in version 11 point 2 so  the 50 points got down to you know under  you know quite a bit faster about a  factor of 5,000 points got down to a  second so now you can include a lot more  labels without sort of worrying nearly  so much about is this gonna kill the  performance of you know the plot that  I'm trying to generate and additionally  at the same time we've sort of improved  some of the layout algorithms so that in  many cases it'll 11.2 is able to fit in  even more points so here's the same set  of points and there's 250 of them that  we've asked to be labeled I haven't  counted to see how many are actually  labeled in each one  but clearly 11.2 is fit more labels into  the plot and additionally if you find a  point that doesn't have a label and it's  going to not show up really well but  when I mouse over there is a tooltip  right there that tells me what that  label would have been you know but it's  you know you can't see it immediately  because it would be colliding with  everything else and so now we'll look at  [Music]  see if I managed to get stuff out of  order one of the specialty visualization  functions which is feature space plot I  didn't accidentally skip yeah that's  just out of order okay  and so feature space plot is a  visualization function that sort of came  out of a lot of the machine learning  work and conceptually it's very simple  at least from my viewpoint which is it  takes your data and runs it through  dimension reduce and so in this case  it's taking a whole bunch of images  about 240 of them one for every country  and it's reducing each of these flags to  a pair of numbers I don't know the  details of how it does this is magic  black magic but it does some interesting  things so once we get down to a pair of  numbers we're then sort of drawing  because these are images we can use them  to self label themselves and so we just  draw the flag everywhere that you know  at each of the points that it came up  with and sort of some of the things that  are sort of interesting is that you get  sort of across the middle lots of  horizontal stripes down here on the  right hand side you get all the British  Commonwealth nations that you know New  Zealand and Australia and I don't even  know what all of them are that have very  similar appearances down here they're  further in the bottom you get a lot of  the flags that contain crosses over here  you have sort of horizontal stripes with  some  in the middle some sort of circular  element and then up here we have the  ones that have sort of a triangle along  the left edge that pokes into the center  of the flag and so that's sort of  interesting and it's being sort of  computed automatically by dimension  reduce using whatever feature extractors  it uses by default if you change the  feature extractor so in this case I'm  using I'm saying that I want the pixels  to be turned into vectors then we get  something that's much more classified  based on what colors are predominant in  each of the flags with blue at the top  coming down to green and then you know  sort of red at the bottom and and so  that's sort of interesting and you don't  just have to use images I mean it just  makes the best demonstrations it can be  sort of arbitrary data and then there's  extractors the deal with audio and  vectors and matrices and text and all  sorts of things and and so now we'll you  know come to geo bubble chart which is  the latest member in the  geovisualization family inversion 11.1  this is fairly straightforward its  geographic data and bubble chart and you  sort of merge them in the obvious way  and so here we have an example with the  largest cities in the United Kingdom and  you know London is by far the largest of  the cities but so the thing is that  because we're leveraging sort of bubble  chart you know it gets all the same  benefits that bubble chart has and so in  particular some of the labeling that I  showed earlier so here we have Miami New  York Seattle and Los Angeles and I've  given each of them a an arbitrary value  and I've said that I want them to be  labeled with call-outs of Miami you know  in Seattle and New York and LA are  abbreviated  and so because event just sort of  natively using everything that it gets  from bubble charts New York and LA have  their labels inside the bubbles and  Seattle and Miami get the call-out and  so if you've used bubble chart  moving on to geo bubble chart for your  geographic data is completely  straightforward and so the final slide  is about an anime plot 3d so we added  this and I think version 11 using a lot  of data that we have on the knowledge  base and it was one of the functions  that at the time we added it we weren't  quite sure what we wanted to do with it  theme wise and so it didn't originally  support the plot team option and so for  11.2 we came back and added that  framework to it and we're able to do  some interesting things with it I think  so the first is that so we have this  detailed theme which gives us axes and  then also gives us tooltips on each of  the different components in the plot  scientific uses unique colors for  everything to help distinguish where one  part ends and another part ends and  again has the tooltips on it which don't  show up terribly well but they're there  and then there's a couple of themes that  are specific to anatomy plot 3d one is  vintage which is sort of historical  rendering ish effect and I think  possibly the colors don't reproduce on  this monitor or this projector very well  the background is much more of a sepia  type color you know a tannish rather  than a blueish gray and then the final  one is sort of a simulated x-ray not  completely truthful or faithful to an  actual x-ray but enough to give you an  idea  I think our x-ray theme still  accentuates some types of tissue that  would be almost completely invisible to  an actual x-ray so that you can still  get some hint of what is there so thank  you and that finishes my talk  [Applause]  you
 

